var SUBMIT_REVIEW_PROMPT = "Review the CURRENT workbook against the user task and original evidence. These are general checks and examples; the task controls.\n\nRead every report page using mode=review, then submit mode=apply against the current report_id with the FULL list of KEEP and INVESTIGATE exceptions. Every omitted supported group automatically RESTORES its original property; an empty exception list restores all supported changes. Omitted unsupported groups remain INVESTIGATE. KEEP and INVESTIGATE both leave the current property untouched; only KEEP resolves review. Each exception needs a concrete reason and evidence; reuse evidence across genuinely identical calculation families. Explicit RESTORE is optional. Read-only requests, implicit stops, malformed submissions and stale reports never trigger default restoration. After any edit or restoration, review the current report and resubmit the full exception list. Use run_apps_script for further reading or editing. RESTORE affects only the named property on exact member cells. Background color is excluded. Use inspect for original/current details and split for a subset.\n\nReview requested work missing from the diff too. In checks, give an obligation, observed evidence, an independent check, and outcome satisfied or unresolved for each relevant family or task section. Empty diffs still require completion evidence. Finish only after every current group is KEEP, all report pages are reviewed and task checks are satisfied. After any restoration, review the refreshed report. The same procedure applies on every submit; the displayed round/time budget is binding.\n\n## Mandatory preservation\n\n**NO FORMATTING CHANGES UNLESS THE TASK REQUIRES THEM.** Do not overwrite, clear, move, or reformat protected or unrelated regions. Leave correct existing formulas and values exactly as they are unless the task explicitly requires their replacement. Do not rewrite an equivalent formula, normalize styles, recolor cells, copy entire formats, or make a section look more consistent merely because you are working nearby. A required content change does not authorize a formatting change: justify each changed property separately against a task requirement. Preserve intentional blanks, spacers, historical data, assumptions, and prefilled formulas; never fill a whole rectangle when only some cells need work. A formula returning an empty string is still content, not a preserved blank. Write only the exact cells and properties needed. Correct output values do not excuse preservation damage. RESTORE unnecessary changes; use INVESTIGATE when the evidence or restoration support is insufficient. KEEP requires evidence that the specific change is necessary and correct.\n\n## R1. Establish source identity; distinguish lookalikes\n\nRequired review questions:\n1. What quantity, entity, period, scenario and measurement basis does the destination require?\n2. Which original source matches that meaning? What rules out the plausible alternative?\n3. Where do the candidates disagree? Verify there rather than choosing a convenient matching period.\n4. What inputs should and should not influence this quantity?\n\n### Example: the counterfactual separates plausible prices\n\nA historical revenue table contains units sold, realized transaction price and today's list price. Both prices give plausible revenue. For already booked revenue, changing today's list price while historical sales stay fixed should not change the answer. That rules out current list price as the valuation basis for the historical sale. Confirm the source labels and period support this interpretation; do not treat this example as a rule for forecast revenue.\n\nWeak review: “Both fields are prices; this multiplication is reasonable.”\nUseful review: “This is booked historical revenue. The selected realized price belongs to the sale period. A change to today's list price does not enter the formula.”\n\n### Example: agreeing forecasts conceal the wrong history\n\nTwo blocks contain “Service costs.” One is the consolidated statement and one an operating build-up. Historical values are 120 versus 135, but both forecast 150. Checking the forecast cannot establish which historical series the destination needs. Read the historical pair and the component formulas/section context. Select the series that matches the destination's scope.\n\nA matching label, matching forecast, no-error result or plausible magnitude is not source proof.\n\n## R2. Verify sign, denominator, units and period locally\n\nDo not import a source's sign convention into a destination without checking its meaning and consumers. Do not apply one convention to an entire workbook. Never use ABS simply to hide a sign mismatch.\n\n### Example: discount amount versus signed change\n\nA reference amount is 80 and a reduced amount is 68. If an existing monetary “saving” is `80-68=12`, a saving rate on the reference base is `12/80=15%`. A signed change `68/80-1=-15%` is a different quantity with the same magnitude. A markup back to 80 is `80/68-1`, with a different denominator again. The task, nearby existing relationship and intended interpretation select the right quantity.\n\nCheck the percentage against its monetary counterpart and denominator where available. Inspect this separately for different ratio families; labels such as “A to B” are not a universal operator specification.\n\n### Example: one economic item has different signed presentations\n\nAn equipment purchase costs 200. A cash-flow row may require -200, while an asset-additions schedule requires +200. An expense schedule may show positive consumption of 20, while an income statement requires a -20 deduction and a cash-flow bridge adds it back. Verify each destination and link; a correct final total does not establish correct intermediate signs.\n\n### Example: two sign mistakes cancel\n\nSuppose working capital is defined as receivables minus payables. Receivables 30 and payables 50 give -20; next period it is -25. The cash release is +5. Reversing the balance to +20 then +25 and reversing the movement calculation can still yield +5. Check the balance definition and the movement independently.\n\n### Example: displayed units can already convert\n\nA stored value of 2,400 in thousands may display as 2.4 million because of its number format. Dividing by 1,000 in the formula and retaining that format converts twice. Check raw value, format and display together. Similarly, do not combine a current-year numerator with a future-year denominator without support from the task or model.\n\n## R3. Keep checks independent; reconcile opening bases\n\nFor each claimed validation, ask: “What possible mistake would make this fail?” If the answer is none because of how you defined the inputs, it is a consistency identity, not independent validation.\n\n### Example: a plug destroys its own detector\n\nAssets are 150, modeled liabilities are 95, and independently supported equity is 50. The balance difference is 5. Setting equity to `150-95=55` forces zero but hides the mismatch. Derive equity from its supported opening balance and movements, then diagnose the remaining 5.\n\nAn explicitly requested residual is legitimate. In that case, do not use the resulting zero as evidence that the other components are correct. Do not invent opening cash, equity or retained earnings merely to obtain a neat reconciliation.\n\n### Example: incompatible opening debt bases\n\nThe historical balance sheet closes debt at 80. A financing table starts at 86. If forecast movement is -5, simply selecting 86-5 instead of 80-5 introduces a persistent 6 difference. Determine whether the table includes a documented transaction, different scope or adjustment; reconcile that difference. Do not silently select a convenient opening base or offset the discrepancy in equity.\n\nA rule about a charge is not automatically a rule about the underlying stock: assigning a noncash interest charge to a particular destination does not by itself justify excluding the loan principal. Review principal, interest and repayment as distinct components.\n\nCheck original closing balance + documented changes = forecast opening/closing balance as applicable. A constant difference across periods is a useful clue to a missing opening component, not a reason to invent an offset.\n\n## R4. Audit complete bridges and rollforwards\n\nWrite down the distinct components of each reconciliation or valuation bridge before accepting the total. Verify component identity, sign, inclusion exactly once, and whether any component is already embedded in another.\n\n### Example: an omitted component propagates into ratios\n\nSuppose a model explicitly defines a bridge as base 500 + obligations 90 - existing cash 40 - incremental cash 30. The total is 520. Computing 550 while correctly displaying the incremental cash separately still omits that component from the total. Any ratio using 550 inherits the error. Fix the first incorrect component/total, then verify its downstream consumers.\n\nConversely, if existing cash already includes the incremental amount, subtracting it again would double-count it. Trace the definitions; do not blindly add every nearby row.\n\n### Example: correct magnitude, wrong component identity\n\nA row for “customer deposits received” is not interchangeable with the sum of all working-capital adjustments, even if they happen to agree in one scenario. Establish each component independently before using its sign or aggregate.\n\n### Example: first period differs from continuation\n\nA forecast opening balance may link to an actual-period closing balance, while later periods link to the previous forecast close. Inspect both formulas. A requirement for a coherent model does not imply identical formula text everywhere.\n\n## R5. Preserve the required representation: formula, literal, blank or error\n\nCorrect current numbers are insufficient when the workbook requires live dependencies. Conversely, formulas are not mandatory for task-specified fixed assumptions or literals.\n\n### Example: live derived result versus frozen answer\n\nIf revenue is 120 and margin 25%, `=Revenue*Margin` preserves the model; pasting 30 disconnects it. Generating formula strings in JavaScript and using setFormulas is appropriate. Using JavaScript for an independent numerical check is also appropriate; that does not require replacing the workbook formulas with values.\n\nDo not disguise a hardcoded derived answer as `=30`. It still lacks the required dependency. An explicit fixed assumption of 30 should remain literal if that is the task's requirement.\n\n### Example: zero versus absent work\n\nA required zero at a cohort's start is different from an unfilled blank. A formula returning an empty string is different from a truly empty cell. Existing N/A markers outside a valid schedule should not be overwritten merely to make a rectangle uniform.\n\nInterpret task exceptions precisely. “Clear numeric data” may include numeric results of formulas, while “clear numeric literals” does not. “Retained earnings” names a metric; it is not a preservation instruction triggered by the word “retained.”\n\n## R6. Audit intended coverage and formula boundaries independently of the diff\n\nDerive a small obligations list from the task and original layout: every requested section, side-input column, initialization, continuation, terminal condition, total and required formatting property. Mark each as edited or already satisfied with evidence. The change report cannot show work that was never done.\n\n### Example: triangular cohort schedule\n\nAssume the task defines no charge in a cohort's start month and a charge of 25 in each of the next two months:\n\n| Cohort | January | February | March |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| January | 0 | 25 | 25 |\n| February | blank | 0 | 25 |\n| March | blank | blank | 0 |\n\nA fill beginning one column after the start can calculate the charges correctly while omitting every required diagonal zero. A fill over the full rectangle can overwrite the pre-start blanks. Test the first eligible cell, diagonal, first continuation, terminal cell and an adjacent ineligible cell. Also check any separate input column feeding the schedule.\n\n### Example: generalized formula is unnecessary or incomplete\n\nA self-locating formula using ROW/COLUMN/OFFSET/INDIRECT may be correct, but a generated explicit reference per cell is often easier to inspect. setFormulas accepts different formula strings in one batch. Check whether relative offsets identify the intended source at each boundary. Do not assume fewer distinct formulas means a better implementation.\n\nDo not rewrite a correct dynamic formula solely because it uses these functions. The acceptance test is semantic correctness and complete coverage, not a blacklist.\n\n## R7. Apply YAGNI to edits; preserve exact requested properties\n\nBefore choosing KEEP for a changed property, ask: **“If we restored the original property, which explicit task requirement would stop being satisfied? Demonstrate that difference. If none, restore it.”**\n\nKEEP requires justification for both necessity and correctness: identify the task requirement, cite original/current evidence showing why restoration would leave that requirement unmet, and give an independent correctness check for the relevant calculation family. “Makes it cleaner,” “looks consistent” and “calculates the same number” do not justify keeping an unnecessary edit. Assess each property separately: a needed content change does not justify a formatting change.\n\nIf the evidence is insufficient to answer, choose INVESTIGATE and read the original/current properties before deciding. If no requirement needs the change, choose RESTORE for the supported property group. If automatic restoration is unsupported, use INVESTIGATE and the normal tools to resolve it.\n\n### Example: equivalent formula rewrite\n\nAn existing `=NetSales*TaxRate` already meets the task. Replacing it with positional references changes entered content unnecessarily. If the current formula is an unnecessary rewrite, choose RESTORE for its content group. If it still matches the original, leave it untouched. An explicitly requested replacement is legitimate.\n\n### Example: blank does not mean missing\n\nAn empty column between two tables may be a spacer. Do not fill or format it merely because its neighbors contain values. Establish a requested output identity before treating the blank as incomplete work.\n\n### Example: copying formatting changes several properties\n\nA task requests one number-format change. Copying the neighboring cell's entire format can also change alignment, font, fill and borders. Change only the requested property. A restore decision must likewise target only the property needing restoration.\n\nExplicit task instructions to replace, clear or reformat existing cells override generic preservation advice. Preserve scope and representation, not every populated cell unconditionally.\n\n## R8. Validate the validator and require evidence before changing accepted work\n\nAn error scan proves only that no recognized formula errors were found. A tie-out may be irrelevant, tautological, or already nonzero in the original workbook. Do not force every row labeled “check” to zero.\n\n### Example: sensitivity grid agrees for the wrong reason\n\nThe main valuation and the center of a sensitivity table both reuse an incorrect cash-flow series. Their agreement confirms consistency, not the cash-flow series. Check a source-driven operating or cash-flow calculation independently before relying on the agreement.\n\n### Example: reviewing correct work into an error\n\nA formula uses historical realized price. On review, current list price seems more “standard.” Do not switch based on that preference. Identify a contradiction with the task, original data or a valid independent relationship before changing the formula. If evidence still supports the existing choice, KEEP it.\n\nReadbacks must support concrete statements: include expected relationship, observed representative values/formulas and outcome. Do not log “verified” without performing a check. A counterfactual may be reasoned algebraically; do not claim to have changed inputs and run a test unless that actually happened safely.\n\n## R9. Use conservation to disambiguate aggregate versus recurring amounts\n\n### Example: a remaining total is not a per-period charge\n\nAn asset balance is 900. The stated first-year charges are 200, 180 and 160, with a remaining bucket of 360 over four later years. Those buckets sum to 900; treating 360 as the charge in EACH later year violates the total. If the model specifies even allocation, the later annual charge is 90. If it specifies another pattern, use that pattern.\n\nCheck cumulative charges against the stated depreciable/amortizable balance, residual and remaining useful life. Verify annual/monthly conversions, remaining-period counts and first/last eligible period. Do not infer straight-line allocation solely from this example.\n\n## R10. Repair the first wrong dependency and verify the affected family\n\nRepeated output errors often come from one source choice, omitted component or sign. Locate that first divergence rather than patching displayed outputs separately.\n\nAfter a correction, verify the direct result and its downstream totals/ratios, plus an initialization or boundary case if relevant. Do not stop after repairing one issue in a run that also has missing cells, representation errors or preservation damage. Keep the task-obligation list separate from the change decisions.\n\n## Required review response\n\nAfter reading every page, use mode=apply with the current report_id and the FULL exception list. KEEP and INVESTIGATE both preserve the current property and require a short reason and evidence:\n\n- KEEP: show why the change is required and why the current result/property is correct.\n- INVESTIGATE: state the unresolved issue and the specific reading or correction needed; perform further work with the normal tools, then submit again.\n- Omitted supported group: RESTORE the original property automatically, only on the exact group members. No justification for the default is required. An explicit RESTORE with reason and evidence is also accepted.\n\nOmitted unsupported groups stay unresolved as INVESTIGATE. Empty exceptions in mode=apply mean restore all supported changes. Never omit a necessary edit merely to shorten the response. Each apply replaces the previous exception list; after edits/restores, review the refreshed report and submit all current exceptions again. Read-only mode=review, implicit stops, malformed submissions, stale report IDs and unread report pages never trigger default restoration.\n\nSuccessful completion requires every remaining group in the current report to be KEEP, every report page reviewed, and all task checks satisfied, including requested work absent from the diff. Restoration produces a refreshed report for review; an unresolved INVESTIGATE cannot finish. An empty diff still requires satisfied task checks.\n\nFor each relevant calculation family, a compact witness such as:\n\n```\nMeaning: historical booked amount, in thousands.\nEvidence: original realized-price source and period headers.\nCheck: changing current list price cannot affect the historical amount;\n       compared source alternatives in a period where their values differ.\nDecision: KEEP [group IDs].\n```\n\nOr:\n\n```\nIssue: the balance check is zero because one component is its residual.\nNeeded evidence: supported opening balance and independent movement schedule.\nDecision: INVESTIGATE [group IDs]; read those sources before editing.\n```\n\nDo not demand a separate invented root cause for every cell. Share a witness across genuinely identical families, while keeping exceptions separate. Unsupported confidence claims and generic “all looks good” are not evidence.\n\n";
